# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Environment template
#
# THE APPLICATION RUNS WITH NO ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES SET.
#
# With no variables set, the app uses the in-memory data adapter and demo
# providers throughout: nothing calls a vendor API and there are no credentials
# to supply. Copy this file to `.env.local` only if you want to pin which client
# a local dev server serves, or to point it at a real database.
#
# Two sections below describe variables the code ACTUALLY READS — "Client
# selection", and "Persistence". Auth is partially real; read its caveat.
# Everything else is COMMENTED OUT and NOT YET IMPLEMENTED: those names are
# illustrative placeholders showing the shape a future adapter would read. Do
# not treat them as an integration contract; the adapter that eventually lands
# picks its own names.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Client selection — the only variable this codebase actually reads
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Read in src/config/active.ts. One deployment serves one client.
#
# Registered slugs (the keys of the registry in src/config/active.ts):
#   template          legacy SiteConfig scaffold; all modules off (marketing only)
#   teen-edge         legacy SiteConfig; booking enabled
#   mentor-academy    legacy SiteConfig; booking + content enabled
#   collective        legacy SiteConfig; community enabled
#   experience-demo   ClientConfig, interactive-experiences preset ("Lantern Rooms")
#
# The four legacy configs are adapted at load by normalizeLegacyConfig(). The
# `content` and `community` modules are foundation-only: they contribute no UI.
#
# Unset falls back to "teen-edge". An unknown slug logs a warning and also falls
# back to "teen-edge" rather than failing the build.

NEXT_PUBLIC_CLIENT=experience-demo


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Provider credentials
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# src/providers/types.ts defines five provider interfaces. Auth has a live
# adapter (Supabase); the other four have only demo adapters, and
# src/providers/index.ts THROWS if a client's `integrations` block requests a
# non-demo commerce/session/media/messaging provider — setting a variable in
# those sections does not enable anything.
#
# See docs/provider-integrations.md for what each production adapter must do.


# --- Auth (AuthProvider) --- PARTIALLY IMPLEMENTED -------------------------
# Demo adapter (default): DemoAuthProvider returns a seeded persona from
# src/data/seed/viewers.ts. There is no sign-in, no session, no authorization.
#
# Supabase adapter: set integrations.auth.provider = "supabase" in the client
# config plus the two variables below AND DATABASE_URL. It verifies a Bearer
# access token against Supabase on every call and resolves roles from OUR
# `users` table, never from token metadata — which is why the database is
# required. Startup fails if any of the three is missing, rather than running a
# deployment that looks configured but signs everybody out.
#
# WHAT IS NOT BUILT: the sign-in flow — no login UI, no OAuth callback route, no
# session refresh, and no provisioning of a `users` row on first login. Until
# those exist, only callers that already hold an access token (API clients,
# server-to-server) can authenticate; a browser visitor is treated as signed out.
#
# The anon key is a publishable client key, not a secret. Do NOT put the
# service-role key in this file or in any NEXT_PUBLIC_ variable — it bypasses
# row-level security.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_URL=https://<project-ref>.supabase.co
# NEXT_PUBLIC_SUPABASE_ANON_KEY=


# --- Commerce (CommerceProvider) --- NOT YET IMPLEMENTED -------------------
# Demo adapter: DemoCommerceProvider returns `demo_charge_*` / `demo_refund_*` /
# `demo_payout_*` references. No card is charged and no payout is scheduled.
#
# COMMERCE_API_KEY=
# COMMERCE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=


# --- Session / live video (SessionProvider) --- NOT YET IMPLEMENTED --------
# Demo adapter: DemoSessionProvider mints a `demo_room_*` id pointing at the
# in-app shell route and a non-functional placeholder token. No room is created
# on any service and there is no media transport.
#
# SESSION_PROVIDER_API_KEY=
# SESSION_PROVIDER_API_SECRET=
# SESSION_PROVIDER_WS_URL=
# TURN_SERVER_URL=
# TURN_SERVER_USERNAME=
# TURN_SERVER_CREDENTIAL=


# --- Media (MediaProvider) --- NOT YET IMPLEMENTED -------------------------
# Demo adapter: DemoMediaProvider maps a sample ref to a local path under
# /public. No CDN, no signing, no remote host.
#
# MEDIA_PROVIDER_TOKEN=
# MEDIA_PLAYBACK_SIGNING_KEY=


# --- Messaging (MessagingProvider) --- NOT YET IMPLEMENTED -----------------
# Demo adapter: DemoMessagingProvider returns { ok: true } and delivers nothing.
# Conversations live in the in-memory repository for the process lifetime.
#
# MESSAGING_PROVIDER_API_KEY=


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Persistence — IMPLEMENTED
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# src/data/repositories.ts defines the repository interfaces. Two adapters
# implement them: the in-memory one (src/data/memory) and Postgres via Drizzle
# (src/data/postgres).
#
# SELECTION IS BY PRESENCE OF DATABASE_URL. Unset -> in-memory, which needs no
# credentials and resets on restart. Set -> Postgres. There is no separate
# toggle, so an accidentally-set DATABASE_URL silently switches adapters; check
# getDataMode() if the data you expect is missing.
#
# Runtime connection. For Supabase use the Supavisor TRANSACTION pooler on port
# 6543 — the client sets `prepare: false` for it, which is required. Serverless
# runtimes open many short-lived connections, and the pooler is what keeps that
# from exhausting Postgres.
#
# DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:6543/postgres

# Migrations only (npm run db:migrate, db:generate, db:studio).
#
# DDL cannot run over the transaction pooler, so this must be a SESSION-mode
# connection: port 5432, either the direct host or the session pooler. Falls
# back to DATABASE_URL if unset, which will fail on port 6543.
#
# MIGRATION_DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.<project-ref>:<password>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres

# Max connections in the runtime pool. Default 5. Keep it small: the ceiling
# that matters is concurrent serverless instances x this number.
#
# DATABASE_POOL_MAX=5


# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Not environment variables
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# These are configured per client in `clients/<slug>/…`, not here:
#   integrations.formEndpoint    lead-capture endpoint (POST /api/lead validates
#                                and acknowledges but delivers nowhere)
#   integrations.analytics       { provider, id } — no analytics script is
#                                currently injected by the app
#   integrations.bookingEmbedUrl third-party appointment embed for legacy clients
